                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "44189249",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198006080692",
                            "num_reviews": 7,
                            "playtime_forever": 1050,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 1002,
                            "last_played": 1535268661
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The Quiet Sleep has zero frills. It isn't for everyone, but it's also one of the smartest games I've ever played and I can't recommend it enough if you enjoy system-driven narrative, which it handles exquisitely. \n\nIt introduces itself with a couple of quick directions, then the training wheels are off and you're all on your own. You might have to stumble your way through things for a while until all the systems start making sense (or at least I definitely did) but you don't need to understand every piece to enjoy it - there are a hundred little ways it will make you stop you every now and then to sit back and go \"OHMYGOD that's so clever!\"\n\nIt's a game that's trying to distill the messiness of human life, and the clear-cut resource management systems using currencies like \"Will\" and \"Energy\" that drive it are a canvas for your imagination to fill in the blanks and the developer to tell his stories. That said, your imagination does need to do a good bit of work, and you have to be willing to draw a story around what is in the large part abstract. A lot of what you get out of it comes from what you put into it, but there's a [I]lot[/I] \n\nWhen you do, things [i]really[/i] start to get good though. It's worth a lot more than it's priced at, and if you enjoy good design, it's definitely something you should give a go.",
